// Format-string errors reported as `Unknown variable` — a PASS-2 diagnostic, so it
// needs a file with no pass-1 error in it.  The parser skips pass 2 the moment pass 1
// reports an error (`Parser::parse`), and `known_var_or_type` only speaks when
// `!first_pass`; sharing a file with 35-format-errors.loft's `Unexpected formatting
// type` (pass 1) is what kept both of these silently unasserted (loft#929).

// A bare unknown specifier has no width digits in front of it, so the parser reads
// `z` as the WIDTH EXPRESSION — a name, and an undefined one.  (With digits, as in
// `{n:5z}`, the specifier grammar owns the `z` and says so directly; that twin lives
// in 35-format-errors.loft.)
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Unknown variable 'z'
fn test_bare_specifier_z() {
  n = 42;
  s = "{n:z}";
}

// A name interpolated BEFORE its assignment is undefined at the point it is read —
// the format string does not defer the lookup to the end of the block.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Unknown variable 'cd'
fn test_format_use_before_assign() {
  s = "{cd}";
  cd = 5;
}
